# Copy to deploy/.env and fill in real values. Both Compose and the Makefile
# read that exact path, so it has to live next to the compose files:
#
#   cp deploy/.env.example deploy/.env
#
# .env holds credentials and must never be committed.
# Generate secrets with: openssl rand -base64 32

# --- Core database -----------------------------------------------------------
CORE_DB_USER=dbcontest
CORE_DB_PASSWORD=change-me-before-first-run
CORE_DB_NAME=dbcontest_core
# Host port for the development overlay only; the stack itself talks over the
# private network.
CORE_DB_PORT=5432

# --- Game database -----------------------------------------------------------
# A second PostgreSQL instance holding one database per participant. Separate
# from the core one so that a runaway query costs the game cluster's CPU and
# memory, never the ones sign-in, answers and the timer depend on.
GAME_DB_USER=dbcontest_game
GAME_DB_PASSWORD=change-me-before-first-run
GAME_DB_NAME=dbcontest_game
# The two roles the Query Runner connects as. Never used by anything else, and
# never handed to a participant: a participant reaches this cluster only
# through the interface.
GAME_READER_PASSWORD=change-me-before-first-run
GAME_WRITER_PASSWORD=change-me-before-first-run
# The role an organiser's game script runs as, and the Core API's own
# credential on this cluster. Required whenever GAME_PROVISIONER_DSN below is
# set; the API refuses to start without it.
#
# It exists because the route that accepts a game script is gated by a
# permission on one contest, while the cluster the script runs on holds every
# other contest's template and every participant's database. Run as the
# provisioning role, a script was arbitrary SQL as a superuser — a shell in the
# container by way of COPY ... FROM PROGRAM — bought with the right to edit one
# draft contest. game_author owns the game's objects and can do nothing else:
# no superuser, no roles, no databases, no files, no programs.
GAME_AUTHOR_PASSWORD=change-me-before-first-run
# cgroup limits for the game cluster container.
GAME_DB_CPUS=2.0
GAME_DB_MEMORY=2g
# Host port for the development overlay only, bound to loopback. The stack
# itself never publishes this cluster.
GAME_DB_PORT=5433
# The provisioning role, used by the Core API to create and drop participants'
# databases. Deliberately not a participant role: the Query Runner is the only
# process that ever connects as game_reader or game_writer. Empty turns
# provisioning off.
GAME_PROVISIONER_DSN=
# How many spare copies each live contest keeps ready, so that somebody
# registering does not wait for CREATE DATABASE. Around a tenth of the expected
# participants.
GAME_POOL_DEPTH=10
# The ceiling on what one contest may ask the cluster to hold. The depth above
# is headroom on top of the roster, so the number of databases a contest asks
# for follows the roster — and a roster imported with a mistake in it would
# otherwise be a request for a database per wrong name. Size it against the
# disk this cluster has. Zero means no cap.
GAME_POOL_MAX=500
# How much disk every database on the game cluster may occupy together — this
# platform's and anything else sharing it. The cap above counts copies, and a
# count says nothing about a disk without the size of one copy: five hundred
# copies is ten gibibytes of a small template and a terabyte of a large one.
# This is the bound that is actually about the volume the cluster sits on, so
# size it against that volume, in bytes. When it binds, the pool stops growing
# and the API logs a line naming the contest, the size of one copy and how
# full the cluster is. Zero means no byte budget.
GAME_CLUSTER_MAX_BYTES=68719476736
# How many copies are made at once. Two to four: enough to fill a pool in
# reasonable time, few enough that filling it is never what the cluster is busy
# doing.
GAME_PROVISION_WORKERS=3
# How long one run of an organiser's game script (in the editor, or an
# uploaded dump) may take to build. Thirty minutes: comfortably past a
# multi-gigabyte dump's COPY blocks on ordinary disk, short enough that a
# build wedged against a stuck cluster is noticed the same day. Raise it for
# an installation whose games are larger than that.
GAME_BUILD_TIMEOUT=30m

# WAL_LOG or FILE_COPY; empty leaves PostgreSQL's own default. Which wins
# depends on the size of the template, so it is a measurement on the real thing
# rather than a constant.

# How long a contest's participant databases survive after it finishes, for
# every contest that never sets its own grace_period_min. A day, so an
# organizer has time to pull reports and a participant finishing an answer at
# the buzzer is not wiped out — see docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §2.4.
GAME_INSTANCE_GRACE_MIN=1440

# --- Uploading a finished dump -----------------------------------------------
# The second way to build a contest's game: instead of writing SQL in the
# editor, an organiser uploads a finished pg_dump — which for a real olympiad
# is gigabytes. It is ON by default, so these five are here rather than only in
# docker-compose.yml: an operator who copies this file as the catalogue of
# knobs would otherwise get the feature, a volume, and a 4 GiB / 16 GiB budget
# on their disk without a line saying any of it exists.
#
# Where the uploads land inside the api container. EMPTY TURNS THE WHOLE
# FEATURE OFF — the same convention GAME_PROVISIONER_DSN and QUERY_RUNNER_ADDR
# above use — and the interface then simply offers no upload control. Set it
# together with the game-uploads-data volume in docker-compose.yml: a
# directory the process cannot write to is a misconfiguration this variable
# alone cannot catch.
GAME_UPLOAD_DIR=/var/lib/dbcontest/uploads
# The largest single dump this installation accepts, in bytes. 4 GiB, above
# the 1-3 GB a pilot names for itself on purpose: the first organiser to
# approach a number somebody guessed at design time should meet a slow upload,
# not a refusal they cannot raise themselves.
GAME_UPLOAD_MAX_FILE_BYTES=4294967296
# Everything the upload directory holds together — uploads in progress, and
# completed ones still waiting to be superseded or reclaimed. 16 GiB. Size it
# against the volume it sits on, the way GAME_CLUSTER_MAX_BYTES is sized
# against the game cluster's disk; an upload announcing more than what is left
# is refused before a byte of it is sent.
GAME_UPLOAD_MAX_DIR_BYTES=17179869184
# The largest single PUT of one chunk, in bytes. 8 MiB. The browser reads this
# number from the API and slices the file by it, and it is also the request
# body ceiling on that route, so raising it raises both together.
GAME_UPLOAD_CHUNK_BYTES=8388608
# How long an upload may sit untouched before the janitor cancels it and frees
# the disk. A day: an organiser stepping away mid-upload does not lose their
# place, and a browser tab closed mid-upload does not hold gigabytes for ever.
GAME_UPLOAD_ABANDONED_AFTER=24h

# --- Query Runner ------------------------------------------------------------
# The separate service that checks and executes participants' SQL. It is the
# only process holding the game cluster's credentials, and it is not reachable
# from outside the private network.
# One query's deadline. This is the bound that holds against SQL the validator
# did not stop: statement_timeout can be turned off from inside a query, a
# deadline on the connection cannot.
QUERY_DEADLINE=5s
QUERY_MAX_ROWS=1000
QUERY_MAX_BYTES=5242880
# How many queries may run at once, and how many may wait. Two to three times
# the game cluster's cores.
QUERY_CONCURRENT=8
QUERY_QUEUE_DEPTH=16
# How often one participant may ask. The semaphore above bounds what is running
# at once; this bounds a rate, which it cannot — a thousand cheap queries pass
# it one at a time.
QUERY_PER_MINUTE=30
# Functions added to the allow-list after a pilot, comma-separated. A refusal
# names the function it refused, and the journal panel groups them, so this is
# what the pilot's findings turn into without a release.
QUERY_EXTRA_FUNCTIONS=

# Adminer (dev database browser): make dev-db-ui, then http://localhost:8081.
ADMINER_PORT=8081

# --- Cache (optional) --------------------------------------------------------
# Leave REDIS_ADDR empty (the normal case) to run without a shared cache: the
# API falls back to its in-process store. That is correct for ONE replica and
# wrong for several — sessions and rate limits stop being shared. To use Redis
# instead, set REDIS_PASSWORD below and start the "shared" compose profile
# (`make dev-up` / `docker compose --profile shared up -d redis`) — both
# `docker-compose.yml` (for the containerized api) and the Makefile (for
# `make run` on the host) derive the right address from REDIS_PASSWORD on
# their own; leaving REDIS_ADDR empty here is what lets each side compute its
# own correct value.
#
# Only set REDIS_ADDR explicitly to point the CONTAINERIZED api at a Redis
# that isn't this compose stack's own `redis` service (an external cache, a
# different port). It has no effect on `make run` — a bare process on the
# host cannot resolve compose network hostnames like `redis` anyway, so the
# Makefile always computes its own host-relative address from
# REDIS_PASSWORD instead of reading this value. See the Makefile comment
# next to REDIS_ADDR for exactly why.
REDIS_ADDR=
REDIS_PASSWORD=change-me-before-first-run
REDIS_PORT=6379

# --- Ingress -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The hostname Caddy serves and obtains a certificate for. Left as "localhost"
# it issues a local certificate itself, so the stack is HTTPS with no setup;
# set a real hostname and it obtains a public certificate automatically.
SITE_ADDRESS=localhost
HTTP_PORT=80
HTTPS_PORT=443

# --- API ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Only used by the development overlay, which exposes the API directly.
API_PORT=8080
ENV=production
LOG_LEVEL=info

# Where request statistics go: prometheus (scrape endpoint), log (periodic
# digest into the log stream) or none.
METRICS_BACKEND=prometheus

# Language of last resort, used when a request expresses no usable preference
# and no contest narrows it down. English by default because the game database
# is English, so it is the one language every installation certainly has.
# Must be a code present in the `languages` table (en, ro, ru are seeded;
# adding a fourth is an INSERT, not a deploy).
DEFAULT_LOCALE=en

# --- Contest timing -----------------------------------------------------------
# Network-latency allowance added to a participant's own deadline (docs/
# ARCHITECTURE.md §8, both timing models) before the Core API refuses a query
# or an answer for arriving too late. Not QUERY_DEADLINE above: that is the
# Query Runner's own per-query execution timeout, a different service and a
# different kind of "deadline" entirely — this is the contest clock's grace,
# consumed by the Core API (queryproxy.Service), never by the Query Runner.
DEADLINE_GRACE=5s

# --- Sessions ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Idle lifetime of a session; it slides on every authenticated request.
SESSION_TTL=12h

# CIDRs whose X-Forwarded-For is trusted when resolving client addresses.
# Defaults to the pinned compose network (Caddy). Add your LB's range if one
# sits in front; leave empty when clients connect directly.
TRUSTED_PROXIES=172.28.0.0/16

# Mark the session cookie Secure. Defaults to true outside ENV=development.
# Leave it on wherever the site is served over HTTPS: a browser will not send a
# Secure cookie over plain HTTP, and will not protect a non-Secure one.
COOKIE_SECURE=true

# --- Interface ---------------------------------------------------------------

# One file for both services. The API reads ENV, the interface reads NODE_ENV,
# and the Makefile derives the host-side addresses from the same values when
# either is run outside a container (`make run`, `make front`).

# Where the interface's server dials the Core API. The default is the API's
# service name on the internal compose network, which is what a single-host
# install wants; change it only when the API runs somewhere else. A process on
# the host uses loopback instead — the Makefile does that translation, the same
# way it does for CORE_DB_DSN.
API_ORIGIN=http://api:8080

# development turns off Next's production behaviour, including the content
# security policy, which is deliberately not sent where it protects nobody.
NODE_ENV=production

# --- Observability (optional) ------------------------------------------------
# Started with: docker compose --profile observability up -d
# Without it, set METRICS_BACKEND=log so request statistics reach the log
# stream instead of an endpoint nobody scrapes.
GRAFANA_PASSWORD=change-me-before-first-run
GRAFANA_PORT=3001

# --- First administrator -----------------------------------------------------
# Used by: docker compose --profile bootstrap run --rm bootstrap
ADMIN_LOGIN=admin
ADMIN_NAME=System Administrator

# VERSION is deliberately not set here.
#
# It names which release runs, and it is passed by the deploy command:
#
#   make deploy VERSION=v1.4.0
#
# An assignment in this file would beat that: the Makefile reads it, and a
# variable defined in a makefile wins over one from the environment. A version
# pinned here would therefore be deployed silently no matter what anybody
# asked for. Raw `docker compose up` without one falls back to the tag "dev",
# which does not exist in the registry — a loud failure instead of a quiet
# wrong deploy.

GAME_COPY_STRATEGY=
# Where the Query Runner answers. Empty turns the SQL console off, which is the
# honest state of a deployment where the runner has not been rolled out — the
# pools are still kept stocked, the endpoint simply is not mounted.

# Front origins this API answers for, on top of its own host, comma separated.
#
# Leave it empty behind the bundled Caddy: it passes the browser's Host through,
# so the API's own host already is the origin the page came from, and the
# cross-origin check needs nothing said. Set it only where that is not true —
# an interface and an API answering on different names, or a development stack
# where the browser is on :3000 and Next forwards /api/* to :8080. An origin
# not listed here is still refused.
PUBLIC_ORIGINS=
